// A task that waits in a call of class suspends can continue, so it prevents a
// deadlock (spec/04-semantics.md, Deadlock). In the first part the main task
// waits in task.receive() while the only other task sleeps. In the second part
// the main task waits while `waiter` waits for a line on a Socket and `feeder`
// sleeps, then sends it: no task is running or ready to run, and no program
// ends with DeadlockError.
// The CHECK lines do not bind the bootstrap interpreter. It runs every worker on
// one host thread, so a blocking call blocks every task (spec/README.md,
// Conformance: scheduling is implementation-defined). The probe on the
// NOT-BINDING line checks the bootstrap's own choice in its place.
// STATUS: ok
// TIMEOUT: 10
// NOT-BINDING: bootstrap bootstrap-only/same_worker_blocking_call.lox
// CHECK: napper woke
// CHECK: waiter got: data
var me = task.self();
fun napper() { sleep(0.2); task.send(me, "napper woke"); }
task.spawn(napper);
print task.receive();

var srv = listen("127.0.0.1", 0);
var peer = connect("127.0.0.1", srv.port());
var conn = srv.accept();
fun waiter() { task.send(me, "waiter got: " + conn.readline()); }
fun feeder() { sleep(0.2); peer.writeline("data"); }
task.spawn(waiter);
task.spawn(feeder);
print task.receive();
